26  # Supabase Management API call sites this guard can still SEE.
    #
    # THIS IS A COVERAGE FLOOR, NOT AN OFFENDER CEILING, AND THE DIRECTION IS INVERTED
    # RELATIVE TO THIS REPO'S OTHER .highwater FILES. lint-diagnosis-claims.highwater and
    # lint-trap-tempfile-ownership's baseline count OFFENDERS, so they ratchet DOWN and their
    # header says "do not RAISE this to make a build pass". This one counts CALL SITES THE
    # EXTRACTOR STILL MATCHES, so:
    #
    #   DO NOT LOWER THIS NUMBER TO MAKE A BUILD PASS.
    #
    # A drop is the failure. It means either a call site was genuinely deleted (fine -- lower
    # this in the SAME commit as the deletion, and say which site) or the extractor went blind:
    # a pathspec typo, a renamed tree, a reworded `API=` assignment, a comment-marker change.
    # A guard that stops matching prints "0 violations" and looks exactly like a clean repo,
    # which is the class 924994b2f closed. This number is the only thing that tells them apart.
    #
    # Growth is legitimate. A new Management API caller RAISES it; the guard prints a note
    # asking you to. Raising is free, lowering needs a reason.
    #
    # WHAT THIS NUMBER COUNTS, EXACTLY. Non-comment lines, in the union of the two assemblies, that
    # match a Management API CALL CONSTRUCT: a host token (the literal `https://api.supabase.com`
    # or a `$VAR`/`${VAR}` used as the host) immediately followed by `/v1/projects`. It is a
    # CENSUS OF THE SCANNED SCOPE, NOT OF THE REPO -- it counts only what the pathspec recorded
    # verbatim in the script header walks, and only what the call-construct anchor detects.
    #
    # THE ANCHOR STOPS AT `projects`; THE TRAILING SLASH IS NOT REQUIRED (corrected 2026-08-27).
    # It used to be, and that demand was a hole rather than a nicety: a caller that builds its
    # base in one statement and appends the path in the next --
    # `BASE="${API}/v1/projects"` then `"$BASE/$REF/<path>"` -- ends the segment on a QUOTE, so
    # the extractor scored it zero and the ratchet certified the silence. Widening the anchor
    # LEFT THIS NUMBER AT 26, which is itself the finding worth recording: no caller in the
    # tree uses the split-base form today, so the correction bought coverage against the shape
    # rather than absolving any existing line. A future split-base caller now RAISES this
    # number instead of being invisible to it.
    #
    # Deliberately NOT counted, each verified by reading the line:
    #   - `/v1/projects/` with no host token (assertion strings in
    #     apply-inngest-rls-dev-workflow.test.sh:169-170, postgrest-reload-schema.test.sh:117)
    #   - comment lines (inngest.tf:218, 0002_dev_inngest_tables_lockdown.sql:60)
    #   - Hetzner's `/v1/servers/`, `/v1/actions/`, `/v1/images/` in scripts/cutover-inngest.sh.
    #     The anchor is `/v1/projects` and not `/v1/` precisely so a Supabase host-pin rule
    #     cannot red three correct Hetzner lines. Dropping the trailing slash does not loosen
    #     that: `projects` is still the discriminating segment, and Hetzner has no such path.
    #   - apps/web-platform/infra/cron-egress-allowlist.txt:66, the bare hostname
    #     `api.supabase.com` with no scheme and no path -- in neither assembly by construction.
    #
    # PROVENANCE, 23 -> 26 (same PR, 2026-08-27). Measured, not estimated. The pre-branch
    # census was 23. This branch adds exactly three Management API call constructs, and the
    # per-file count reconciles the total exactly:
    #
    #   scripts/supabase-logs-query.sh              2   (the logs query + the instrumentation
    #                                                    enumeration; the host pin is a bare
    #                                                    literal here BY DESIGN, so that this
    #                                                    new client is a member of the very
    #                                                    assembly it ships alongside)
    #   tests/scripts/test-supabase-logs-query.sh   1   (the fake-curl seam's expected URL)
    #
    # An intermediate commit on this branch set 25, counting only the two sites in the helper
    # and missing the third in its suite. Corrected at integration by re-running --census after
    # all three workstreams had landed, which is exactly what the paragraph below demands and
    # is the reason it demands it: the number was measured by a workstream that could not see
    # the whole branch.
    #
    # Recording the widening here rather than silently editing the digit is the discipline
    # lint-diagnosis-claims.highwater established: a number that moves without a reason
    # attached stops being evidence of anything.
    #
    # RE-MEASURED AT INTEGRATION, AND THIS INSTRUCTION IS DISCHARGED. An earlier revision of
    # this paragraph told the lead to re-run --census before merge, and named 25 while doing
    # so. Both are now stale: the re-run happened once all three workstreams had landed, it
    # printed 26, and that is the committed value. The instruction is recorded rather than
    # deleted because it did real work -- it is why the 25 was caught.
    #
    # The standing rule it leaves behind: any commit that adds a Management API caller RAISES
    # this number in the same commit. Leaving it low is not "safe" -- slack on a coverage
    # floor is exactly the silent slack this ratchet replaced.
    #
    # The three `advisors/security` sites inside this census are WAIVED, not absent. The waiver
    # lives in the script's DENY array with its date and justification; removing it is what
    # turns this population red, and that is the guard's live non-vacuity proof.
